1
00:00:00,120 --> 00:00:02,222
LEGO says a brick made in nineteen

2
00:00:02,289 --> 00:00:05,350
fifty-eight still snaps
onto a brick made today.

3
00:00:05,780 --> 00:00:09,404
That's a promise about precision,
kept for more than sixty years.

4
00:00:09,471 --> 00:00:10,720
Remember that promise.

5
00:00:11,150 --> 00:00:16,440
This is the story of how a company famous
for precision lost count of its own parts,

6
00:00:16,507 --> 00:00:18,420
and how it got the count back.

7
00:00:18,850 --> 00:00:23,560
In two thousand and three, LEGO couldn't
tell you what its own boxes cost.

8
00:00:23,990 --> 00:00:26,683
One of its designers
later said some sets cost

9
00:00:26,750 --> 00:00:29,330
more to source than
the whole box sold for.

10
00:00:29,760 --> 00:00:31,620
And no one at LEGO actually knew.

11
00:00:32,050 --> 00:00:35,200
The next year, LEGO lost
almost two billion kroner.

12
00:00:35,630 --> 00:00:38,150
Bankers advised the family
to sell the company.

13
00:00:38,580 --> 00:00:42,720
Twenty years later, LEGO was bigger
than Mattel and Hasbro combined.

14
00:00:43,150 --> 00:00:47,173
So how did LEGO nearly die? Everyone
says it nearly went bankrupt,

15
00:00:47,240 --> 00:00:49,773
and was saved by going back to the brick.

16
00:00:49,840 --> 00:00:55,040
The real story is stranger. It never
went bankrupt, and no hit toy saved it.

17
00:00:55,470 --> 00:00:56,970
It was saved by counting.

18
00:00:57,653 --> 00:01:01,713
It started with wood, in a carpenter's
workshop in the Danish village of Billund.

19
00:01:02,143 --> 00:01:06,023
In nineteen thirty-two, Ole Kirk
Christiansen began making wooden toys.

20
00:01:06,453 --> 00:01:09,453
He took the name from two Danish
words, leg godt: play well.

21
00:01:09,893 --> 00:01:13,523
His motto, from nineteen thirty-six:
only the best is good enough.

22
00:01:13,953 --> 00:01:17,373
For its first twenty-eight years,
LEGO was a wood company.

23
00:01:17,803 --> 00:01:22,310
Plastic came after the war, and LEGO's
early bricks followed a British design,

24
00:01:22,377 --> 00:01:24,253
Kiddicraft's self-locking brick.

25
00:01:24,683 --> 00:01:27,883
What LEGO added wasn't the
brick. It was a system.

26
00:01:28,313 --> 00:01:31,953
In nineteen fifty-five, Ole's son
Godtfred launched the System of Play.

27
00:01:32,383 --> 00:01:35,663
So every new set would work with
every set a child already owned.

28
00:01:36,093 --> 00:01:39,453
But the bricks didn't grip yet. The
fix came in nineteen fifty-eight.

29
00:01:39,873 --> 00:01:44,192
On the twenty-eighth of January, LEGO
filed a patent: tubes inside the brick,

30
00:01:44,259 --> 00:01:45,113
so it clutches.

31
00:01:45,543 --> 00:01:47,193
Ole Kirk died a few weeks later.

32
00:01:47,743 --> 00:01:51,613
In February nineteen sixty,
a fire hit the wooden-toy workshop.

33
00:01:52,043 --> 00:01:55,563
Godtfred stopped making wooden
toys, and his brothers left.

34
00:01:56,003 --> 00:01:58,826
From then on LEGO made
one thing: plastic bricks,

35
00:01:58,893 --> 00:02:01,133
in limited shapes and limited colours.

36
00:02:01,563 --> 00:02:04,383
Remember that limit. It's
what made the system work.

37
00:02:04,820 --> 00:02:06,839
The system took off.
By nineteen sixty-six,

38
00:02:06,906 --> 00:02:08,700
LEGO was sold in forty-two countries.

39
00:02:09,120 --> 00:02:10,334
In nineteen sixty-eight,

40
00:02:10,401 --> 00:02:14,670
so many visitors turned up at the factory
that LEGO built them a park, LEGOLAND.

41
00:02:15,100 --> 00:02:18,079
The first season drew six hundred
and twenty-five thousand people,

42
00:02:18,146 --> 00:02:19,300
about twice the estimate.

43
00:02:19,730 --> 00:02:21,980
Then in nineteen seventy-eight
came the minifigure.

44
00:02:22,400 --> 00:02:25,200
For forty-six years, LEGO's
worlds had had no people in them.

45
00:02:25,630 --> 00:02:28,861
In nineteen seventy-nine, the founder's
grandson, Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen,

46
00:02:28,928 --> 00:02:29,470
took charge.

47
00:02:29,910 --> 00:02:32,533
And by the nineties, LEGO
was saying yes to everything.

48
00:02:32,600 --> 00:02:36,840
More parks. Clothes. Watches. Video games.

49
00:02:37,270 --> 00:02:39,260
New themes, new worlds,
new pieces every year.

50
00:02:39,690 --> 00:02:41,381
Designers went from about six thousand

51
00:02:41,448 --> 00:02:43,530
different parts to more
than twelve thousand.

52
00:02:43,960 --> 00:02:47,180
And about ninety percent of the
components were used in just one design.

53
00:02:47,610 --> 00:02:51,290
A system where nothing gets reused
isn't a system. It's a catalogue.

54
00:02:51,710 --> 00:02:53,757
Every new shape needs its own steel mould,

55
00:02:53,824 --> 00:02:56,340
and a mould can cost tens
of thousands of dollars.

56
00:02:56,770 --> 00:02:59,200
In nineteen ninety-eight,
LEGO posted a loss.

57
00:02:59,630 --> 00:03:02,880
In nineteen ninety-nine it signed
its first outside licence: Star Wars.

58
00:03:03,310 --> 00:03:07,010
Star Wars made money. But many
of LEGO's own new worlds did not.

59
00:03:07,453 --> 00:03:09,496
Then the floor gave way.

60
00:03:09,563 --> 00:03:14,273
Then, in two thousand and three,
LEGO's sales fell by more than a quarter.

61
00:03:14,703 --> 00:03:20,063
Its own annual report opened: two thousand
and three was a very disappointing year.

62
00:03:20,493 --> 00:03:24,073
LEGO lost nine hundred and
thirty-five million kroner.

63
00:03:24,503 --> 00:03:28,896
That June, the head of strategy, Jorgen
Vig Knudstorp, put a warning in writing.

64
00:03:28,963 --> 00:03:30,926
KNUDSTORP: On a burning platform,

65
00:03:30,993 --> 00:03:34,308
losing money with negative
cash flow and a real risk of

66
00:03:34,375 --> 00:03:37,996
debt default which could lead
to a break up of the company.

67
00:03:38,063 --> 00:03:40,543
He had joined LEGO less
than two years before.

68
00:03:40,973 --> 00:03:46,203
Then Kjeld took back day-to-day control,
and his deputy, Poul Plougmann, was fired.

69
00:03:46,633 --> 00:03:48,713
And two thousand and four was worse.

70
00:03:49,133 --> 00:03:53,663
LEGO lost one billion, nine hundred
and thirty-one million kroner.

71
00:03:54,103 --> 00:03:57,463
Its main bank stopped lending,
Bloomberg later reported.

72
00:03:57,883 --> 00:04:02,063
Equity fell from about four point nine
billion kroner to under three billion.

73
00:04:02,483 --> 00:04:07,911
On the thirtieth of November two thousand
and four, at a family house in the forest,

74
00:04:07,978 --> 00:04:10,463
bankers from Morgan Stanley met Kjeld.

75
00:04:10,893 --> 00:04:15,718
According to one business-school case,
they recommended selling LEGO,

76
00:04:15,785 --> 00:04:18,763
for about one point seven billion dollars.

77
00:04:19,193 --> 00:04:20,393
The family said no.

78
00:04:21,387 --> 00:04:23,597
But here's the twist the
usual story leaves out.

79
00:04:24,027 --> 00:04:27,990
In that worst year, the toy business
itself, before write-downs, made a profit.

80
00:04:28,057 --> 00:04:29,647
One hundred and three million kroner.

81
00:04:30,077 --> 00:04:34,380
The giant loss came mostly from
cleaning up: write-downs, restructuring,

82
00:04:34,447 --> 00:04:36,207
and closing what didn't work.

83
00:04:36,637 --> 00:04:40,587
The bricks weren't the problem. LEGO
had no way to cost a single set.

84
00:04:41,027 --> 00:04:43,887
Then designers finally went
through the sets, one by one.

85
00:04:44,307 --> 00:04:48,123
The worst were sets with a tiny
motor or fibre-optic lights:

86
00:04:48,190 --> 00:04:51,427
their parts cost more than
the whole box sold for.

87
00:04:51,867 --> 00:04:53,935
One study, in the book Brick by Brick,

88
00:04:54,002 --> 00:04:57,937
later did the maths for nineteen
ninety-three to two thousand and two.

89
00:04:58,367 --> 00:05:02,395
LEGO reported profits in almost
every one of those years,

90
00:05:02,462 --> 00:05:06,910
yet destroyed about one point
six billion US dollars of value.

91
00:05:06,977 --> 00:05:09,997
Profits on paper. Value going down.

92
00:05:10,417 --> 00:05:14,874
That's the complexity tax: every new
piece charges the whole system, the mould,

93
00:05:14,941 --> 00:05:17,347
the colour, the supplier, the shelf space.

94
00:05:17,787 --> 00:05:21,697
When the tax is bigger than what a
piece earns, growth makes you poorer.

95
00:05:22,120 --> 00:05:24,318
In October two thousand and four,

96
00:05:24,385 --> 00:05:28,633
Kjeld stepped back after twenty-five
years as chief executive.

97
00:05:28,700 --> 00:05:31,930
The new chief executive was the
man who had written the warning.

98
00:05:32,360 --> 00:05:33,850
Knudstorp was thirty-five,

99
00:05:33,917 --> 00:05:38,110
and the first chief executive from
outside the family in a generation.

100
00:05:38,540 --> 00:05:41,863
He said his first job wasn't
strategy. It was cash.

101
00:05:41,930 --> 00:05:46,039
KNUDSTORP: At first I actually said,
let's not talk about strategy,

102
00:05:46,106 --> 00:05:50,780
let's talk about an action plan,
to address the debt, to get the cash flow.

103
00:05:51,220 --> 00:05:55,390
The family's holding company, KIRKBI,
lent LEGO eight hundred million kroner.

104
00:05:55,810 --> 00:05:59,190
Not a gift. A loan, with interest,
due in twenty eleven.

105
00:05:59,620 --> 00:06:03,040
Costs were cut by a fifth, more
than one and a half billion kroner,

106
00:06:03,107 --> 00:06:04,720
and about a thousand jobs went.

107
00:06:05,150 --> 00:06:07,870
The parts library was cut
back to about seven thousand.

108
00:06:08,300 --> 00:06:10,170
So the colours were cut roughly in half.

109
00:06:10,600 --> 00:06:13,440
And from then on, a new
part had to earn its place.

110
00:06:13,870 --> 00:06:15,223
Then the parks went too.

111
00:06:15,290 --> 00:06:17,417
In July two thousand and five,

112
00:06:17,484 --> 00:06:23,260
LEGO sold control of LEGOLAND for three
hundred and seventy-five million euros.

113
00:06:23,690 --> 00:06:27,230
LEGO and its family holding
company kept thirty percent.

114
00:06:27,660 --> 00:06:30,955
In two thousand and five,
LEGO made a profit again:

115
00:06:31,022 --> 00:06:33,790
five hundred and five million kroner, net.

116
00:06:34,220 --> 00:06:38,710
Sales grew only a little. The swing
came from what LEGO stopped doing.

117
00:06:39,153 --> 00:06:41,523
What came next is the
comeback everyone remembers.

118
00:06:41,953 --> 00:06:43,051
In twenty fourteen,

119
00:06:43,118 --> 00:06:48,083
The LEGO Movie took about four hundred
and sixty-eight million dollars worldwide.

120
00:06:48,513 --> 00:06:51,393
The sequel, five years later,
made less than half of that.

121
00:06:51,823 --> 00:06:55,013
But here's the thing: the movies
weren't the comeback. Hits come and go,

122
00:06:55,080 --> 00:06:56,573
and the system is what compounds.

123
00:06:57,003 --> 00:06:58,180
By twenty twenty-five,

124
00:06:58,247 --> 00:07:01,753
LEGO's revenue reached eighty-three
and a half billion kroner.

125
00:07:02,183 --> 00:07:05,043
That's nearly twelve times its
two thousand and five level.

126
00:07:05,473 --> 00:07:06,456
And year after year,

127
00:07:06,523 --> 00:07:10,353
between a quarter and a third of its
sales came back as operating profit.

128
00:07:10,783 --> 00:07:15,143
So, back to that brick from
nineteen fifty-eight. It still fits.

129
00:07:15,573 --> 00:07:18,155
LEGO didn't nearly die
from a lack of ideas.

130
00:07:18,222 --> 00:07:22,013
It nearly died because it stopped
counting what each idea cost.

131
00:07:22,443 --> 00:07:26,585
And it survived because the people
who owned it could afford to lend,

132
00:07:26,652 --> 00:07:27,993
and chose not to sell.

133
00:07:28,423 --> 00:07:32,200
So here's the lesson: when a company
you love keeps adding things,

134
00:07:32,267 --> 00:07:35,353
ask one question. Can anyone
say what each one costs?

135
00:07:35,783 --> 00:07:40,823
LEGO's next test isn't plastic,
because its newest pieces are games,

136
00:07:40,890 --> 00:07:46,823
films and promises about carbon, and none
of them comes in a box you can count.

137
00:07:47,253 --> 00:07:49,988
Can the company that learned to count

138
00:07:50,055 --> 00:07:53,320
every single brick learn
to count those too,

139
00:07:53,387 --> 00:07:57,173
before its catalogue grows
too big all over again?

140
00:07:57,603 --> 00:08:01,662
So, if you had been the family
in two thousand and four,

141
00:08:01,729 --> 00:08:04,462
with the bankers' advice on the table:

142
00:08:04,529 --> 00:08:09,613
would you have sold LEGO to the
highest bidder, or lent it the money?

143
00:08:10,043 --> 00:08:13,173
Tell us in the comments,
and tell us which set you'd keep.

